{"id":133091830046766,"type":"album","name":"From Enslavement To Obliteration","artist":"Napalm Death","year":1988,"weight":14,"info":{"cover":"https://dn710108.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-523aebca-1324-4499-b053-d65a5505ab72/mbid-523aebca-1324-4499-b053-d65a5505ab72-39170254645.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":22,"country":"GB","scaruffi":7.0,"yt":"8ccef0zAMbE","links":{"spotify":"https://open.spotify.com/album/4veFj7IvUM9q3Z0S0A4y0F","youtube":"https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_mR8rxJ9nx6uLN4oEsti_SpSZj99GMbD4s","bandcamp":"https://napalmdeath.bandcamp.com/album/from-enslavement-to-obliteration","rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/napalm-death/from-enslavement-to-obliteration"},"desc":"From Enslavement to Obliteration is the second studio album by English grindcore band Napalm Death, released in 1988. It is the final studio album with vocalist Lee Dorrian and guitarist Bill Steer, and the first to feature bassist Shane Embury, the band's longest-tenured member to date. A remastered version was released on 2 April 2012. Loudwire put it on the list of the 10 best metal albums of 1988. The album's lyrical themes cover a variety of social and political topics, including misogyny/sexism (\"It's a M.A.N.S World\" and \"Inconceivable?\"), animal rights (\"Display to Me…\"), racism (\"Unchallenged Hate\" and \"From Enslavement ."},"artist_ref":{"id":113840725305581,"name":"Napalm Death","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/HF2022NapalmDeath_3.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":31482837869539,"type":"genre","name":"Punk","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":2333,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":99593642367688,"type":"genre","name":"Grindcore","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":217,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"}],"lists":[{"id":165490733860358,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Grindcore","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":39,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":520